<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>



<chapter xml:id="ch-num-int" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Recombination for Numerical Integration</title>

  <introduction>
    <p> This is my contribution to a group project on exploring the applications of smarter subsampling using <url href="https://github.com/datasig-ac-uk/baryx" visual="baryx"/></p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- include sections -->

  <section xml:id="sec-num-int-intro">
    <title>Introduction to Recombination</title>
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-">
        <title></title>
        
        <p>
        Given a dataset of <m>n</m> samples, random subsampling reduces computational cost but introduces Monte Carlo noise and may distort important statistical properties such as means, variances, and correlations. Recombination, also known as measure reduction, instead constructs a reduced-complexity discrete measure <m>\hat{\mu}</m> from a larger measure <m>\mu</m> while preserving specified integral properties of the original measure \cite{litterer2012high}.</p>
        
        <p>
        <c>baryx</c> is a Python/JAX-oriented package for recombination \cite{coxon2026baryx}. It replaces many equally weighted points with a smaller set of unequally weighted points while preserving user-specified statistics exactly, up to selected higher-order moments within numerical tolerance. The library provides efficient, hardware-accelerated tools for this purpose, with a particular emphasis on barycentre preservation, which plays a fundamental role in areas such as computational statistics, optimal transport, and numerical integration.</p>
        
        <p>
        This report forms part of a broader group effort to explore applications of the <c>baryx</c> library, covering data compression with feature preservation, statistical bootstrapping via random subsampling, and numerical integration. Here, we consider the last of these applications: the use of recombination for numerical integration.</p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-num-int">
        <title>Numerical Integration</title>
<p>        Constructing efficient quadrature and cubature rules has long been a central challenge in computational mathematics \cite{engels1980numerical}. Many problems of practical interest lack closed-form solutions, among them integral transforms, ODE solution operators, and cumulative probability distributions. In the Finite Element Method, for instance, quadrature must be evaluated repeatedly over many elements, making efficiency especially desirable even on complex or high-dimensional domains. In this report, we explore the use of the recombination algorithm in <c>baryx</c> to improve quadrature efficiency while preserving accuracy and expressiveness.
</p><p>
A concise, general view of numerical integration is to "approximate, then integrate": a perspective that also encompasses Monte Carlo methods \cite{nakatsukasa2018approximate}. 
Given an integrand <m>f:\Omega\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</m>, we construct an approximate polynomial <m>p\in\mathbb{P}^d</m> through interpolation, and integrate <m>p</m> exactly using a quadrature rule. While the first step is treated as given, this report focuses on the second: constructing efficient quadrature rules for <m>p</m>.</p>
<p>
The action of recombination in this context is to compress the quadrature rule while preserving its degree of precision. That is, given a quadrature rule <m>Q</m> with degree of precision (DoP) <m>d</m> satisfying
<md number="yes" xml:id="eqn-quadrature-1">
    \label{eqn:quadrature-1}
    Qp = \sum_{i=1}^N w_i p(x_i) = \int_\Omega p(x) \ \mathrm{d}x,
    \qquad \forall p \in \mathbb{P}^d,
</md>
recombination finds a reweighted subset <m>\{x_{i_1}, \ldots, x_{i_M}\} \subset \{x_1, \ldots, x_N\}</m> such that <xref ref="eqn-quadrature-1"/> still holds \cite{tchernychova2016caratheodory}.</p>
<p>
We note that this reweighted subset can only provide a quadrature rule of the same or lower DoP of the original rule. 
Thus, a fundamental consideration in the implementation of <c>baryx</c> is to begin with an overdetermined quadrature rule of sufficiently high DoP, which recombination can then compress into a smaller, equally precise rule.</p>

<p>We demonstrate that recombination performs well on complex 2D domains, reducing any cubature of DoP <m>d</m> to <m>(d+1)(d+2)/2</m> nodes. In higher dimensions, recombination effectively compresses Cartesian-product Gaussian quadrature rules even on simpler domains such as the hypercube. These results motivate the use of <c>baryx</c>, an efficient implementation of recombination, in resource-intensive applications such as the Finite Element Method.
</p>
<p>The remainder of this report is organised as follows. In Section 2, we provide background on numerical integration and quadrature rules in one and two dimensions. In Section 3, we develop a general framework for applying recombination to quadrature rules and demonstrate it on simple and complex 2D domains. We derive an upper bound on the node count in terms of the target DoP, and show how suitable modifications that exploit symmetry improve this bound for odd DoP. Finally, we extend the original bound to higher-dimensional domains.
</p>
    </subsection>
  </section>




<section xml:id="sec-num-int-prelim">
    <title>Preliminaries</title>
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-Introduction">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        
    <p>
The aim of numerical integration is to approximate the integral
<md number="yes">
    I = \int_\Omega f(x) \ \mathrm{d}x
</md>
where <m>\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^d</m> (predominantly <m>d=1,2</m>) and  <m>f:\Omega\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</m>.
We focus on quadrature rules, which approximate the integrand by a polynomial <m>p(x) \in \mathbb{P}^d</m> and integrate <m>p</m> exactly:
<md number="yes">
    Qp = \sum_{i=1}^N w_i p(x_i) = \int_\Omega p(x) \ \mathrm{d}x \approx I.
</md>
A quadrature rule has an associated degree of precision (DoP) <m>d</m>, meaning all polynomials of degree <m>\leq d</m> are integrated exactly. 
In this section, we provide a background on constructing quadrature rules to a specified DoP. We begin with the well-understood 1D case before turning to the more involved problem of quadrature in 2D.</p>
    </subsection>


<subsection xml:id="subsec-quad">
    <title>Quadrature in 1D</title>

    <subsubsection>
        <title>Introduction</title>
        
    <p>
In the following, we take <m>\Omega = [-1,1]</m>, so <m>I = \int_{-1}^1 f(x) \mathrm{d}x</m>.
For a quadrature rule to have DoP <m>d</m>, we require <m>Qp = Ip</m> for all <m>p\in\mathbb{P}^d</m>. By linearity of integration, it suffices to check this over a basis of <m>\mathbb{P}^d</m>. A natural choice is the monomial basis <m>\{1, x, x^2, \ldots, x^d\}</m>, resulting in the \textit{momentum equations} \cite{engels1980numerical} (also referred to as moment matching equations \cite{tchernychova2016caratheodory})
<md number="yes" xml:id="eqn-quad-sys-1">
    \label{eqn:quad-sys-1}
    Qx^k = \sum_{n=1}^{N} w_n x_n^k = c_k,
    \qquad \forall k \in \{0, \ldots, d\},
</md>
where <m>x_1, \ldots, x_N</m> are distinct evaluation points, <m>w_1, \ldots, w_N</m> are their associated weights and 
<md number="yes" xml:id="eqn-cases">
    \label{eqn:cases}
    c_k = Ix^k = \begin{cases}
        \frac{2}{k+1} \quad &amp;k \text{ even}\\
        0 \quad &amp;k \text{ odd}.
    \end{cases}
</md>
Writing <xref ref="eqn-quad-sys-1"/> explicitly, we have
<md number="yes" xml:id="eqn-quad-sys-2">
    \label{eqn:quad-sys-2}
    \begin{aligned}
        w_1 + w_2 + \cdots + w_N &amp;= c_0, \\
        w_1 x_1 + w_2 x_2 + \cdots + w_N x_N &amp;= c_1, \\
        w_1 x_1^2 + w_2 x_2^2 + \cdots + w_N x_N^2 &amp;= c_2, \\
        &amp;\vdots \\
        w_1 x_1^{d} + w_2 x_2^{d} + \cdots + w_N x_N^{d} &amp;= c_d .
    \end{aligned}
</md>
The task of constructing a quadrature rule with DoP <m>d</m> is to solve this system for <m>N</m> evaluation points and weights.
</p>

    </subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-fixed-nodes">
    <title>Fixed Nodes</title>
    
<p>We first consider fixed evaluation points. Setting <m>N=d+1</m> results in an exactly-determined linear system in the weights <m>w_1, \ldots, w_N</m>, as in <xref ref="eqn-quad-sys-1"/>. This results in a quadrature rule of DoP <m>d</m> with <m>N=d+1</m> nodes.
</p><p>
A special case arises through considering an odd number of symmetric evaluation points. Suppose we have <m>2N+1</m> points <m>x_{-N}, \ldots, x_0, \ldots, x_N</m>, with <m>x_0 = 0</m> and <m>x_{-n} = -x_{n}</m> for all <m>n\in\{1, \ldots, N\}</m>. The system becomes
<md number="yes" xml:id="eqn-special-fixed-quad">
    \label{eqn:special-fixed-quad}
    Qx^k = \sum_{n=-N}^{N} w_n x_n^k = c_k
    \qquad \forall k \in \{0, \ldots, 2N\}.
</md>
By further imposing symmetric weights <m>w_{-n} = w_n</m>, the odd-power equations are automatically satisfied, since <m>w_{-n}x_{-n}^{2k+1} = -w_{n}x_{n}^{2k+1}</m> and <m>w_0x_0^{2k+1} = 0</m>, consistent with <xref ref="eqn-cases"/>. The even-power equations reduce to 
<md number="yes" xml:id="eqn-special-fixed-quad-2">
    \label{eqn:special-fixed-quad-2}
    Qx^{2k} = w_0x_0^{2k} + \sum_{n=1}^{N} 2w_n x_n^{2k} = c_{2k}
    \qquad \forall k \in \{0, \ldots, N\},
</md>
where the <m>N+1</m> unknowns <m>w_0, \ldots, w_N</m> can be determined exactly from <m>N+1</m> linear equations. Thus, the derived weights <m>w_{-N}, \ldots, w_N</m> solve <xref ref="eqn-special-fixed-quad"/> with DoP <m>2N</m>. Moreover, as all odd-power integrands zero out, the rule in fact achieves DoP <m>2N+1</m>.</p>

<p>Thus, for <m>2N+1</m> evaluation points, enforcing weight symmetry achieves DoP <m>2N+1</m>. In the case of equidistant points, this derives Newton-Cotes quadrature rule, while for Chebyshev points, this gives us the Clenshaw-Curtis quadrature rule \cite{clenshaw1960method}.
</p>
</subsubsection>


<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-free-nodes">
    <title>Free Nodes</title>
    <p>
We return to the momentum equations <xref ref="eqn-quad-sys-2"/> and consider the case where both nodes and weights are free parameters. We show that the nodes can be chosen as roots of a suitable polynomial, which underpins the construction of Gaussian quadrature rules~\cite{engels1980numerical}.</p>
<p>
We want to find <m>2N</m> unknowns: nodes <m>x_1,\ldots,x_N</m> and weights <m>w_1,\ldots,w_N</m>. Let <m>q(x)</m> be the unique monic polynomial of degree <m>N</m> with roots <m>x_1, \ldots, x_N</m>: 
<md number="yes">
    q(x) = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x + \beta_2 x^2 + \cdots + \beta_{N-1} x^{N-1} + x^N .
</md>
Multiplying the <m>k</m>th momentum equation in <xref ref="eqn-quad-sys-2"/> by <m>\beta_k</m> for each <m>k\in\{0,\ldots,N\}</m> gives us
<md number="yes">
    \beta_k w_1 x_1^k + \cdots + \beta_k w_N x_N^k = \beta_k c_k, 
    \qquad k\in\{0,\ldots,N\}.    
</md>
Adding these equations together for all <m>k\in\{0,\ldots,N\}</m>, we have
<md number="yes">
    \begin{aligned}
        &amp; w_1 \bigl( \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_1 + \cdots + \beta_{N-1}x_1^{N-1} + x_1^N \bigr) \\
        &amp;\quad + w_2 \bigl( \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_2 + \cdots + x_2^N \bigr) \\
        &amp;\quad \vdots \\
        &amp;\quad + w_N \bigl( \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_N + \cdots  + x_N^N \bigr)
        = \beta_0 c_0 + \cdots + \beta_{N-1} c_{N-1} + c_N .
    \end{aligned}
</md>
Since <m>q(x_i)=0</m> for all <m>i</m>, the left-hand side vanishes, arriving at
<md number="yes">
    0 = \beta_0 c_0 + \cdots + \beta_{N-1} c_{N-1} + c_N .
</md></p>
<p>
Repeating this argument for shifts <m>j = 0, 1, \ldots, N-1</m> - multiplying the <m>k+j</m>th momentum equation by <m>\beta_k</m> and summing - we arrive at the linear system
<md number="yes">
    \begin{aligned}
        \beta_0 c_0 + \cdots + \beta_{N-1} c_{N-1} + c_N &amp;= 0, \\
        \beta_0 c_1 + \cdots + \beta_{N-1} c_N + c_{N+1} &amp;= 0, \\
        &amp;\vdots \\
        \beta_0 c_{N-1} + \cdots + \beta_{N-1} c_{2N-2} + c_{2N-1} &amp;= 0 ,
    \end{aligned}
</md>
or in matrix form,
<md number="yes">
    \begin{pmatrix}
        c_0 &amp; \cdots &amp; c_{N-1} \\
        \vdots &amp; \ddots &amp; \vdots \\
        c_{N-1} &amp; \cdots &amp; c_{2N-2}
    \end{pmatrix}
    \begin{pmatrix}
        \beta_0 \\
        \vdots \\
        \beta_{N-1}
    \end{pmatrix}
    =
    -
    \begin{pmatrix}
        c_N \\
        \vdots \\
        c_{2N-1}    
    \end{pmatrix}.
</md>
This determines the unique set of coefficients <m>\beta_0, \ldots, \beta_{N-1}</m>, from which the roots <m>x_1, \ldots, x_N</m> of <m>q(x)</m> are found. With the nodes determined, the weights follow from previous argument for <xref ref="eqn-quad-sys-2"/> with fixed nodes.
</p><p>
The above process constructs Gauss-Legendre quadrature, with nodes <m>x_1, \ldots, x_N</m> found to be roots of <m>N^{\text{th}}</m> degree Legendre polynomial~\cite{engels1980numerical}. More generally, introducing a weight function <m>\omega(x)</m> into the integrand leads to other Gaussian quadrature rules, with nodes given by roots of the corresponding polynomials. This includes Gauss-Chebyshev (Chebyshev polynomials), Gauss-Jacobi, Gauss-Laguerre, and Gauss-Hermite quadrature~\cite{engels1980numerical}.
</p>
</subsubsection>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-cubature">
    <title>Cubature in 2D</title>
    
<p>
We now consider cubature rules for domains <m>\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^2</m>. Constructing efficient cubature rules in 2D is significantly more challenging than in 1D and remains an active area of research~\cite{bauman2020compressed}.
We demonstrate this with the example of the "plus" domain, composed of unit squares (<xref ref="fig-triangle"/>).</p>


<figure xml:id="fig-triangle">

  <caption>Diagram of simple 2D quadrature of DoP 1 on the plus domain.</caption>

  <image xml:id="tikz-1">
    <latex-image>

    <!-- CDATA prevents certain LaTeX code from being interpreted as xml -->

  \tikzset{%
    block/.style    = {draw, thick, rectangle, minimum height = 3em,
      minimum width = 3em},
    sum/.style      = {draw, circle, node distance = 2cm}, % Adder
    input/.style    = {coordinate}, % Input
    output/.style   = {coordinate} % Output
  }
  % Defining string as labels of certain blocks.<!--
  \newcommand{\suma}{\Large$+$}
  \newcommand{\inte}{$\displaystyle \int$}
  \newcommand{\derv}{\huge$\frac{d}{dt}$}-->
  

    \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.2]

    % ---------- left square ----------
    \draw (0,1) -- (1,1) -- (1,0) -- (0,0) -- cycle;
    \draw (0,0) -- (1,1);

    \node[above left]  at (0,1) {$c$};
    \node[below left] at (0,0) {$b_1$};

    % ---------- center square ----------
    \draw (1,1) -- (2,1) -- (2,0) -- (1,0) -- cycle;
    \draw (1,0) -- (2,1);

    \node[above left] at (1,1) {$b_3$};
    \node[below left] at (1,0) {$b_2$};

    % ---------- right square ----------
    \draw (2,1) -- (3,1) -- (3,0) -- (2,0) -- cycle;
    \draw (2,0) -- (3,1);

    \node[above right] at (2,1) {$b_2$};
    \node[above right] at (3,1) {$b_1$};
    \node[below right] at (3,0) {$c$};

    % ---------- top square ----------
    \draw (1,2) -- (2,2) -- (2,1) -- (1,1) -- cycle;
    \draw (1,1) -- (2,2);

    \node[above left] at (1,2) {$c$};
    \node[above right] at (2,2) {$b_1$};

    % ---------- bottom square ----------
    \draw (1,-1) -- (2,-1) -- (2,0) -- (1,0) -- cycle;
    \draw (1,-1) -- (2,0);

    \node[below left] at (1,-1) {$b_1$};
    \node[below right] at (2,0) {$b_3$};
    \node[below right] at (2,-1) {$c$};

    % ---------- nodes as dots ----------
    \foreach \x/\y in {
    0/0,0/1,1/0,1/1,2/0,2/1,3/0,3/1,1/2,2/2,1/-1,2/-1}
    {
    \fill (\x,\y) circle (1.5pt);
    }

    \end{tikzpicture}
    </latex-image>

  </image>

</figure><p>
A natural starting point is geometric construction~\cite{engels1980numerical}. In <xref ref="fig-triangle"/> the plus domain is tessellated with 10 identical triangles, and a quadrature rule is constructed by assigning each vertex a weight proportional to the total area of incident triangles. Vertices labelled <m>c</m> are incident to one triangle, <m>b_1</m> to two, <m>b_2</m> to 4 and <m>b_3</m> to five, giving
<md number="yes">
    Qf = \frac{|\Omega|}{10} \cdot \frac{1}{3} \cdot 
    \left(\sum_{\mathbf{x_c}\in c} f(\mathbf{x_c}) + 
    2\sum_{\mathbf{x}_{b_1}\in b_1} f(\mathbf{x}_{b_1}) + 
    4\sum_{\mathbf{x}_{b_2}\in b_2} f(\mathbf{x}_{b_2}) + 
    5\sum_{\mathbf{x}_{b_3}\in b_3} f(\mathbf{x}_{b_3})\right).
</md>
Here, <m>|\Omega|/10</m> is the area of each triangle, and each triangle contributes the average of <m>f</m> at its three vertices. This rule has DoP 1 by construction, integrating linear functions exactly, but not quadratics.</p>
<p>
This is a far cry from the efficiency of quadrature rules in 1D. In Gaussian quadrature, <m>N</m> nodes yield DoP <m>2N-1</m>, whereas here 12 nodes achieve only DoP 1.
Treating these as fixed nodes, 12 weights can satisfy 12 momentum equations. Since DoP <m>d</m> requires <m>(d+1)(d+2)/2</m> momentum equations, this corresponds to at most DoP 3.
</p><p>
However, the difficulty of constructing efficient cubature rules in 2D is due to the potential complexity of the domain. Unlike a line segment, momentum integrals
<m>c_{\alpha\beta} = \int_\Omega x^\alpha y^\beta \mathrm{d}x\mathrm{d}y</m>
over a general <m>\Omega\subset\mathbb{R}^2</m> may be difficult or impossible to evaluate analytically, making it impractical to assemble the momentum equations <xref ref="eqn-quad-sys-2"/> needed to find suitable nodes and weights.
</p>
<p>
For rectangular domains, higher DoP can be achieved by extending 1D quadrature rules along each axis via Cartesian products~\cite{tchernychova2016caratheodory} (also called tensor products~\cite{trefethen2017cubature}). For a Gaussian quadrature rule <m>Q</m> with DoP <m>d \ge \max\{k_1, k_2\}</m>, we have that
<md number="yes">
    \begin{aligned}
        \int_a^b\int_u^v x^{k_1}y^{k_2} \mathrm{d}x\mathrm{d}y &amp;= \int_a^b x^{k_1} \mathrm{d}x \int_u^v y^{k_2} \mathrm{d}y \\
        &amp;= Qx^{k_1} Qy^{k_2} \\
        &amp;= \sum_{n=1}^{N}\sum_{m=1}^{N} w_n w_m x_n^{k_1}  y_m^{k_2} =: \tilde{Q}x^{k_1}y^{k_2}.
    \end{aligned}
</md>
This yields a cubature rule <m>\tilde{Q}</m> with DoP <m>2N-1</m> on the rectangular domain <m>[a,b]\times[u,v]</m> using <m>N^2</m> nodes. We will refer to this as Gaussian cubature. </p>

<p>One option for complex domains is to tessellate with rectangles and apply Gaussian cubature to each, giving a patchwork of cubature rules, as illustrated in <xref ref="fig-gaussian"/>. Although it has been shown that Gaussian cubature rules can be constructed directly for any polygonal domain~\cite{sommariva2007product}, tessellation remains a practical approach for non-polygonal, irregular or disconnected domains.
</p>
